1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
Let us build one up.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:09,275
Start with two special states that emit nothing and exist only for the transitions: Begin and End, the blue spheres.

3
00:00:09,275 --> 00:00:14,675
Begin leads into the first state of the profile; everything eventually leads to End.

4
00:00:14,675 --> 00:00:16,775
Then the match states.

5
00:00:16,775 --> 00:00:22,375
N is not the number of residues in a sequence, and it is not the length of the alignment.

6
00:00:22,375 --> 00:00:28,650
It is the number of columns in the alignment where most of the sequences have a residue rather than a gap.

7
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:34,725
Match-to-match transitions run in one direction only, from each state to the next — like time.

8
00:00:34,725 --> 00:00:37,875
From Begin, through the match states, to End.

9
00:00:37,875 --> 00:00:44,825
A model with only match states cannot handle gaps, so we add delete states, one for every match state.

10
00:00:44,825 --> 00:00:53,650
That gives three kinds of transition: delete to delete, for a run of gaps; match to delete, where a gap begins; and delete to match, where it ends.

11
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:57,025
For extra residues we add insert states.

12
00:00:57,025 --> 00:01:02,675
Since an insertion can occur at either end, there are N plus one of them, numbered from zero.

13
00:01:02,675 --> 00:01:12,975
Again three kinds of transition: match to insert where an insertion begins, insert to match where it ends, and insert to itself for consecutive inserted residues.

14
00:01:12,975 --> 00:01:18,050
For completeness there are also transitions between insert and delete states.

15
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:20,625
They are rare, but they are possible.

16
00:01:20,625 --> 00:01:26,550
And finally the emissions: from each match state, the residues actually observed in that column.

17
00:01:27,150 --> 00:01:30,300
And the emissions from the insert states too.

18
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:38,575
A hidden Markov model adds something a scoring matrix does not have: the probability of moving from one state to the next.

19
00:01:38,575 --> 00:01:45,875
From a match state, the model can go to the next match state — the ordinary case of one aligned residue following another.

20
00:01:46,475 --> 00:01:52,600
From a delete state, the model can carry on deleting, or rejoin the alignment at the next match.

21
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:58,575
A run of deletions is how the model represents a gap in the query relative to the family.

22
00:01:59,175 --> 00:02:06,825
From an insert state, the model can insert again — which is why the insert state loops back on itself — or return to matching.

23
00:02:06,825 --> 00:02:10,775
A loop is how the model allows an insertion of any length.

24
00:02:11,375 --> 00:02:19,575
Each match state also emits a residue, with a probability for each of the twenty amino acids, or four bases for DNA.

25
00:02:19,575 --> 00:02:26,350
Those emission probabilities are the profile part of the model: they are the column frequencies in another notation.

26
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:35,700
Insert states emit too, usually with the background frequencies, because an inserted residue is not expected to follow the family's pattern.

27
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:42,475
Now the model is built from an alignment, and the first decision is which columns count as match states.

28
00:02:43,075 --> 00:02:49,225
The rule of thumb is occupancy: a column where most sequences have a residue is a match state.

29
00:02:49,825 --> 00:02:53,500
A column that is mostly gaps is not a match state.

30
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:57,400
Whatever residues it holds are treated as insertions.

31
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,725
Going along the alignment, each column is labelled one way or the other.

32
00:03:03,325 --> 00:03:10,275
That labelling fixes the length of the model: the number of match states is the number of columns that survived the test.

33
00:03:10,875 --> 00:03:14,250
Every residue in the alignment now has a role.

34
00:03:14,250 --> 00:03:18,075
In a match column it is an emission from that match state.

35
00:03:18,675 --> 00:03:25,050
In an insert column it is an emission from the insert state that sits between the neighbouring match states.

36
00:03:25,650 --> 00:03:30,375
And a gap in a match column is a visit to that position's delete state.

37
00:03:30,975 --> 00:03:35,675
Sequence by sequence, the alignment is read as a path through the model.

38
00:03:36,275 --> 00:03:42,675
Each path is a series of states — match, insert, delete — from the beginning state to the end state.

39
00:03:43,275 --> 00:03:48,225
Different sequences take different paths, and the paths are what get counted.

40
00:03:48,825 --> 00:03:52,850
A short sequence with gaps takes a path through delete states.

41
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:58,600
A sequence with extra residues takes a path that loops through an insert state.

42
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:07,125
Once every sequence has a path, the model is trained simply by counting how often each transition and each emission was used.

43
00:04:07,725 --> 00:04:11,550
Here is the alignment again, with the paths it implies.

44
00:04:12,150 --> 00:04:15,075
Counting begins at the first position.

45
00:04:15,675 --> 00:04:19,050
Every transition out of a state is counted.

46
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:22,675
So is every residue emitted from it.

47
00:04:23,275 --> 00:04:26,775
The counts accumulate as the alignment is read.

48
00:04:27,375 --> 00:04:32,650
And when the reading is done, each state has a tally of what left it and what it emitted.

49
00:04:33,250 --> 00:04:36,625
Dividing by the total gives a probability.

50
00:04:36,625 --> 00:04:41,800
Four sequences out of five taking the same transition gives nought point eight.

51
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,425
The remaining fifth gives nought point two to the alternative.

52
00:04:47,025 --> 00:04:54,300
Every state's outgoing transitions sum to one, which is what makes this a probability model rather than a score table.

53
00:04:54,900 --> 00:05:02,000
And a transition nobody took gets zero — the same problem that minus infinity caused in the scoring matrix.

54
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:06,300
A zero probability says the path is impossible.

55
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:12,225
For a model trained on five sequences, that is a much stronger claim than the data supports.

56
00:05:12,825 --> 00:05:19,000
So the counts are smoothed, exactly as before, by adding a small amount to each before dividing.

57
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,300
The same is done for the emissions.

58
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:28,250
A residue never seen at a position becomes unlikely rather than impossible.

59
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:34,800
How much to add is a judgement, and the usual answer scales it with how little data there is.

60
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:41,250
With few sequences, the pseudocounts dominate and the model stays close to the background.

61
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:46,675
With many sequences, the counts dominate and the model follows the family.

62
00:05:47,275 --> 00:05:56,150
Notice this one: no sequence has a deletion after that insertion, so the probability of going from insert six to delete six is zero.

63
00:05:56,750 --> 00:06:04,400
Same point on this transition: no sequence has a deletion after that insertion, so that probability is zero too.

64
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:10,850
The smoothed probabilities replace the raw ones throughout the model.

65
00:06:11,450 --> 00:06:13,625
Transitions first.

66
00:06:14,225 --> 00:06:16,850
Then emissions, state by state.

67
00:06:17,450 --> 00:06:24,050
And the model is complete: a length, a set of states, and a probability on every arrow and every letter.

68
00:06:24,650 --> 00:06:29,300
Compare the two sets of numbers and the effect of smoothing is visible.

69
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:34,850
Where a raw probability was nought point eight, the smoothed one is a little lower.

70
00:06:35,450 --> 00:06:38,950
Where it was zero, it is now small but not zero.

71
00:06:39,550 --> 00:06:42,025
Nothing has been invented.

72
00:06:42,025 --> 00:06:46,725
The ordering is unchanged; only the certainty has been reduced.

73
00:06:47,325 --> 00:06:56,750
This is the trained model drawn out: match states along the top, insert states above them, delete states below, and the beginning and end states at either side.

74
00:06:57,350 --> 00:07:04,175
Every arrow carries a probability, and every match and insert state carries a distribution over residues.

75
00:07:04,175 --> 00:07:06,350
That is the whole model.

76
00:07:06,950 --> 00:07:11,300
With a model in hand, there are two things to do with a new sequence.

77
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:13,675
The first is to score it.

78
00:07:14,275 --> 00:07:26,900
Scoring an aligned sequence means following its path through the model and multiplying the probabilities along the way — or, in practice, adding their logarithms, because the products become very small very quickly.

79
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:31,700
Each matched residue contributes its emission probability.

80
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:36,125
Each step contributes its transition probability.

81
00:07:36,725 --> 00:07:41,125
A gap contributes the cost of entering and staying in delete states.

82
00:07:41,725 --> 00:07:45,000
An insertion contributes the cost of the loop.

83
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:53,600
Add them all and the result is a score for this sequence against this family, usually compared against the score under a background model.

84
00:07:54,200 --> 00:08:03,000
The second thing is harder and more useful: given a sequence and no alignment at all, find the path through the model that the sequence most likely took.

85
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:13,050
That is what the Viterbi algorithm does, and it is dynamic programming again — the same principle as Needleman-Wunsch, with states instead of matrix cells.

86
00:08:13,650 --> 00:08:16,825
The table is filled one position at a time.

87
00:08:17,425 --> 00:08:21,825
Each cell holds the best score for reaching that state at that position.

88
00:08:22,425 --> 00:08:29,325
Reaching a state means coming from one of the states that can precede it, and the algorithm keeps only the best of those.

89
00:08:29,925 --> 00:08:34,800
It also records which one it was, so the path can be recovered afterwards.

90
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:38,600
Filling continues to the end of the sequence.

91
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:43,150
The best score at the end state is the score of the best path.

92
00:08:43,750 --> 00:08:53,525
And tracing the recorded choices backwards gives the alignment: which residues matched which positions, where the insertions went and which positions were deleted.

93
00:08:54,125 --> 00:08:57,775
To close, the three methods in one sentence each.

94
00:08:57,775 --> 00:09:03,425
A word search finds a local match quickly, and treats every position of the query alike.

95
00:09:03,425 --> 00:09:10,675
A profile search learns from a family what each position tolerates, and finds relatives a single sequence would miss.

96
00:09:10,675 --> 00:09:19,525
And a hidden Markov model adds the probability of the gaps themselves, which is what makes it the most sensitive of the three — and the slowest to build.

